    {# ===================== 4 — POSITIONING =====================
      One line, centred, then three figures under it, with nothing to click.
      This is the only section on the page with no next step in it, which is
      the point: it is the argument, and an argument that ends in a button
      reads as a pitch.
    #}
    <section class="section section--light hp-position" id="positioning" aria-labelledby="hp-position-line">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="hp-position__inner">
          {# "The track record behind every job" (Matt, 14/10/26). The old line,
             "Water treatment isn't optional. Neither is doing it properly.", made
             a point about the industry, so it did not introduce the figures
             under it. This section is the proof beat between the services and
             the work, and the heading now says the figures are that proof. #}
          <h2 class="statement hp-position__line" id="hp-position-line">The track record <span class="em">behind every job.</span></h2>
          {# NUMBERS, NOT A PARAGRAPH (Matt, 14/10/26). Three figures the site
             already stands behind: the firm's age (site.stats, and the
             "twenty years" claims elsewhere), the 24-hour line, and the
             credentials list's "all water analysis goes through a
             UKAS-accredited lab". NOT site.stats' "200+ sites under our care",
             which is still unconfirmed by the client.

             They count up from zero together as the row scrolls into view (count-up.js).
             The real figure is in the markup, so with no JS or with reduced
             motion it simply shows; screen readers get the figure once, from
             the visually hidden copy, rather than every step of the count. #}
          {% set positionStats = [
            { to: 20,  suffix: "+", label: "Years in business" },
            { to: 24,  suffix: "h", label: "Emergency line" },
            { to: 100, suffix: "%", label: "UKAS-accredited testing" }
          ] %}
          <ul class="hp-stats" role="list" data-count-group>
            {% for s in positionStats %}
            <li class="hp-stat">
              <p class="hp-stat__figure"><span class="visually-hidden">{{ s.to }}{{ s.suffix }}</span><span aria-hidden="true" data-count-to="{{ s.to }}" data-count-suffix="{{ s.suffix }}">{{ s.to }}{{ s.suffix }}</span></p>
              <p class="hp-stat__label">{{ s.label }}</p>
            </li>
            {% endfor %}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# ===================== 6 — SELECTED WORK =====================
      A CAROUSEL OF EVERY STUDY, one at a time: photograph left, the account
      right. It used to be Cowdray alone, on the argument that one job told
      properly beats three at card size. That still holds per slide; the
      carousel just lets the other seven be told the same way, without eight
      screens of page. Cowdray leads, as the one named client.

      Arrows sit out in the page gutters, level with the photograph, and step
      one study at a time (carousel.js). Below 900px they go and the track is a
      swipe with dots. With no JavaScript it is a plain snapping scroller.

      Slides carry the first body paragraph only. The full write-ups run to
      three paragraphs, and a slide that changes height by 200px on every
      click makes the section below it jump.
    #}
    {% set serviceNames = {
      "private-water-supplies": "Private water supplies",
      "design-install": "Design and install",
      "water-hygiene": "Water hygiene",
      "servicing": "Servicing"
    } %}
    {% set cowdray = {
      id: "cowdray",
      title: "Cowdray Estate, <span class='em'>one supply serving many properties.</span>",
      location: "Midhurst, West Sussex",
      services: ["private-water-supplies"],
      body: ["Cowdray needed a reliable, high-volume private supply serving multiple properties and estate operations. We surveyed two potential borehole sites, specified the higher-yield source, and installed a treatment system built around iron and manganese reduction, pH correction to stop the water corroding pipework, and UV sterilisation."],
      highlight: "Running continuously since 2023, and passing UKAS-accredited testing every year.",
      blurb: "A high-volume supply for several properties and estate operations.",
      image: "/assets/img-9257.webp",
      imageAlt: "A red brick water tower beside a barn with solar panels, with an Invicta van on the drive."
    } %}
    {# GROUPED BY SERVICE, in the order we want them read (Matt, 20/10/26):
       private supplies first as the main service, servicing next, then design
       and install, then water hygiene. Each group keeps the file's order, and
       Cowdray still opens. Sorted here so the service pages, which read the same
       file, keep their own order. #}
    {% set pool = [cowdray].concat(caseStudies) %}
    {% set slides = [] %}
    {% for svc in ["private-water-supplies", "servicing", "design-install", "water-hygiene"] %}
      {% for c in pool %}{% if c.services[0] == svc %}{% set _ = slides.push(c) %}{% endif %}{% endfor %}
    {% endfor %}
    <section class="section section--paper hp-work" id="selected-work" aria-labelledby="hp-work-title">
      <div class="container">
        <div class="hp-work__head">
          <div>
            <h2 class="statement hp-work__title" id="hp-work-title">Supplies we’ve designed, <span class="em">installed and looked after.</span></h2>
          </div>
          <p class="hp-work__count" aria-hidden="true"><span data-cases-current>01</span> / {% if slides.length < 10 %}0{% endif %}{{ slides.length }}</p>
        </div>

        <div class="hp-cases" role="region" aria-roledescription="carousel" aria-label="Case studies">

          {# ARROWS PINNED LEFT AND RIGHT, JUST OUTSIDE THE CONTENT (Matt,
             14/10/26). They started out in the window's own gutters, which on a
             wide screen put "next" at the far right edge; then they moved in
             beside the dots, which was close but read as a control panel under
             the slide. Now they sit either side of the stage, level with the
             middle of the photograph, one arrow's width outside the content
             edge. The stage wrapper exists so "the middle" means the slide's
             middle, not the slide plus the dots row under it. #}
          <div class="hp-cases__stage">
            <button type="button" class="hp-cases__arrow hp-cases__arrow--prev" data-cc-prev aria-label="Previous case study"><svg viewBox="0 0 56 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M55 12H2M12 2L2 12l10 10"/></svg></button>
            <button type="button" class="hp-cases__arrow hp-cases__arrow--next" data-cc-next aria-label="Next case study"><svg viewBox="0 0 56 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.25" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M1 12h53M44 2l10 10-10 10"/></svg></button>
          <div class="hp-cases__track">
            {% for c in slides %}
            {% set svc = c.services[0] %}
            <article class="hp-case" role="group" aria-roledescription="slide" aria-label="{{ loop.index }} of {{ slides.length }}">
              <figure class="hp-case__media">
                <img src="{{ c.image or '/assets/img-0428.webp' }}" alt="{{ c.imageAlt }}" width="1536" height="1024" loading="{{ 'eager' if loop.first else 'lazy' }}" decoding="async">
                {# THE PLACE SITS ON THE PHOTOGRAPH, as its caption. For a
                   regional business "Midhurst, West Sussex" is real
                   information, but as a line above the title it read as one
                   more eyebrow on a page that had six. #}
                <figcaption class="hp-case__place">{{ c.location }}</figcaption>
              </figure>
              <div class="hp-case__copy">
                {# TITLE, ONE LINE, ONE BUTTON (Matt, 14/10/26: "that right-hand
                   section is just way too complicated"). It carried a place
                   label, the title, a 50-word paragraph, a bold highlight and
                   the button. The paragraph and highlight are still on the
                   service pages' case studies; here the one-line blurb does the
                   job, and the button is where the rest of the story is. #}
                <h3 class="hp-case__title">{{ c.title | safe }}</h3>
                {# The problem, then what we did about it, at body size (Matt,
                   14/10/26: "a simple font with a bit more body copy, but the
                   body copy in a smaller size"). #}
                <p class="hp-case__body">{{ c.blurb }}{% if c.highlight %} <span class="hp-case__more">{{ c.highlight }}</span>{% endif %}</p>
                <p class="hp-case__link">
                  <a class="btn-line" href="/services/{{ svc }}/">{{ serviceNames[svc] }} <span class="btn-line__arrow" aria-hidden="true"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 5l7 7-7 7"/></svg></span></a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </article>
            {% endfor %}
          </div>
          </div>
          <div class="dots"></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    {# ===================== 6b — IN THEIR WORDS =====================
      Three real clients, anonymised to the place, as Stefan promised them
      (see _data/testimonials.js). Straight after the one case study, so the
      page goes from our account of a job to the clients' account of theirs.

      NOT A CAROUSEL ON DESKTOP. Three quotes fit on one screen, and a slider
      would hide two of them behind a control to show fewer at once. The
      longest, most specific quote takes the large card; the other two stack
      beside it, so uneven lengths read as a hierarchy rather than as three
      boxes with ragged bottoms. On a phone the same three become a swipe row
      with dots (nav.js TRACKS), which is where a carousel earns its keep.
    #}
    <section class="section section--light hp-voices" id="testimonials" aria-labelledby="hp-voices-title">
      <div class="container">
        <h2 class="statement hp-voices__title" id="hp-voices-title">Don’t just take our word for it.</h2>

        <div class="hp-voices__grid">
          {% for t in testimonials %}
          <figure class="voice{% if t.feature %} voice--feature{% endif %}">
            <svg class="voice__mark" viewBox="0 0 32 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path fill="currentColor" d="M0 24V14.4C0 6.2 4.3 1.4 12.2 0l1.4 3.1C9.2 4.6 7 7.4 6.8 11.2H13V24H0zm19 0V14.4C19 6.2 23.3 1.4 31.2 0l1.4 3.1c-4.4 1.5-6.6 4.3-6.8 8.1H32V24H19z"/></svg>
            <blockquote class="voice__quote">
              {% if t.pull %}<p class="voice__pull">{{ t.pull }}</p>{% endif %}
              {% for para in (t.excerpt or t.quote) %}<p>{{ para }}</p>{% endfor %}
            </blockquote>
            <figcaption class="voice__by">
              <span class="voice__who">Private water supply, near {{ t.place }}</span>
              <span class="voice__scope">{{ t.scope }}</span>
            </figcaption>
          </figure>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

